From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mathieu GRENET <mathieu@mgth.fr>
Date: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 09:23:29 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 09/29] player: built-in spatial overlay client (replaces
 omniphony-overlay.lua)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The overlay used to be driven by a user-installed Lua script that pulled
the finished ASS payload from liborender over LuaJIT's FFI. That made it
silently unavailable on every build using PUC Lua — including the
released Windows and Linux bundles, where lua52 has no ffi module — and
required a manual install into the user scripts directory.

Replace the script with a built-in client: when the orender feature is
enabled, mp_orender_overlay_start() creates a weak client named
"omniphony_overlay" with its own thread that pulls
orender_overlay_ass() / orender_overlay_heatmap_bgra() at 30 Hz and
issues osd-overlay / overlay-add, redraws immediately on osd resize, and
exposes the same controls as the script:

    KEY script-binding omniphony_overlay/<name>
    KEY script-message omniphony-overlay <name>

(The script-binding spelling uses an underscore: mp_new_client rewrites
non-alphanumeric characters, so the dashed client name documented for
the Lua era never actually resolved — script-message was the only form
that worked.)

mpv links liborender directly (DT_NEEDED), so the client calls the
symbols declared in orender.h; no FFI, no dlopen, no Lua flavour
dependency, nothing to install.

Verified with a spatial TrueHD stream: ASS scene + heatmap drawn during
playback, all toggles respond over script-binding and script-message,
clean teardown on quit.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
